<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the Pilot application bundle (PR-042).

  NOTHING IN THIS FILE HAS EVER BEEN APPLIED. No `.app` has been signed, no
  hardened-runtime process has ever started, and `codesign` has never run in
  this repository. Every claim below is reasoning from Apple's documentation,
  not a measurement — `docs/handoff.md` §1 step 22 is the check that turns any
  of it into a fact.

  Pilot is NOT App-Sandboxed. ScreenCaptureKit over an arbitrary user-chosen
  window, system-wide Accessibility and a global push-to-talk event tap are all
  outside what the sandbox grants, and there is no entitlement that buys them
  back. Access is gated by TCC prompts instead, which is the protection the user
  actually sees and can revoke.

  Consequence, recorded because it is easy to miss: with no sandbox there is no
  `com.apple.security.inherit`, so the spawned helper's access to the microphone
  and the screen depends on macOS attributing it to this bundle through the
  *responsible process* relationship rather than on an entitlement.
  `build/entitlements.helper.plist` is the hedge for the case where it does not.

  Each key, and why it is here:

    allow-jit                        Chromium's JavaScript engine. Electron does
                                     not start under the hardened runtime
                                     without it.
    allow-unsigned-executable-memory Same engine, same requirement; V8 writes
                                     and then executes generated code.
    device.audio-input               The microphone. Push-to-talk records audio
                                     in the helper, and a hardened-runtime
                                     process is denied the device without this
                                     even after the user has said yes.

  Each key that is deliberately ABSENT, which matters as much:

    disable-library-validation       Not needed: electron-builder signs every
                                     embedded framework with the same identity
                                     that signs the app, and the helper is
                                     spawned as a process, not loaded as a
                                     library. Adding it would let any
                                     differently-signed dylib into Pilot's
                                     address space for no benefit.
    automation.apple-events          Pilot sends no Apple Events. Accessibility
                                     is `AXUIElement`, which is a different TCC
                                     service with no entitlement and no usage
                                     string. See the note beside
                                     `NSAppleEventsUsageDescription` in
                                     electron-builder.yml.
    files.user-selected.read-write   No file is ever opened or written on the
                                     user's behalf. The conversation database
                                     lives in the app's own container.
    network.client                   Not a hardened-runtime entitlement (it is
                                     an App Sandbox one) and so has no effect
                                     here; the provider requests are ordinary
                                     outbound sockets.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
